Alex (CSM): FYI — onboarding call with Signet just finished. They ramped a browser-based integration to ~100 concurrent clients and we started seeing a burst of 429s during SDK init. They reported most failures happen within the first 10s after startup. Ticket: CS-54321

Jordan (Support Eng): Can you paste a snippet of the logs / client user-agent? 429 pattern often looks like preflight fanout from ephemeral tokens.

Alex (CSM): Log snippet they sent:```
2026-03-18T15:02:11Z INFO http incoming preflight /v1/session 200 user=signet-app UA=SignetWeb/1.2
2026-03-18T15:02:11Z WARN rate_limiter allow=false key=preflight:ephemeral:region-us count=115 window=1s
2026-03-18T15:02:11Z ERROR http 429 token_pool_exhausted
```

Priya (DevEx): That count=115 in 1s is the giveaway — looks like all clients prefetching ephemeral tokens at once. Do they use default SDK prefetch on page load?

Alex (CSM): Yes, they said they call `init()` on every page load and fetch ephemeral tokens in parallel for auth. Single-page nav still triggers init on some widgets.

Maya (Solutions Eng): Suggestion: add stochastic jitter + splay on init to avoid thundering herd. Also ensure server-side token re-use for same session id. Quick mitigation: set SDK option `ephemeralPrefetch: false` and have the app fetch a single token per session and share it across tabs.

Jordan (Support Eng): Also recommend client-side backoff + exponential retry for 429s. Example snippet we gave them:```js
// simple splay/backoff for init attempts
let attempts = 0;
function initWithBackoff(){
  attempts++;
  sdk.init().catch(err=>{
    if(err.status===429 && attempts<5){
      const jitter = Math.random()*500; // 0-500ms
      const backoff = Math.pow(2, attempts)*100 + jitter;
      setTimeout(initWithBackoff, backoff);
    }
  });
}
initWithBackoff();
```

Alex (CSM): I told them to roll that change to staging this afternoon. They asked if we can change server-side rate window for preflight tokens for this tenant temporarily.

Jordan (Support Eng): We can increase tenant burst quota for 24h if Sales ok with it. But prefer app change + edge guidance first. If they need immediate relief, we can bump `preflight.per_tenant.burst` in the token service for CS-54321. Need approval from Maya for config change and ops scheduling.

Maya (Solutions Eng): I'm fine doing a short-lived quota bump (limit from 100/s -> 400/s) for 12h. Will coordinate with eng-oncall to monitor. Please confirm time window.

Alex (CSM): They want it ASAP — starting ~16:00 UTC today for a couple hours while they validate client fix.

Jordan (Support Eng): @deploy-bot can you open a short-lived quota change runbook and notify eng-oncall channel?

deploy-bot: created change request CR-7894 (preflight quota bump for tenant signet, 16:00-18:00 UTC). Notifying #eng-oncall.

Priya (DevEx): Added a brief FAQ snippet for integrations: "If you see 429s on SDK init during heavy client ramp, check for parallel ephemeral token fetches and enable jitter/splay. Prefer single token per session and share across widgets." I'll push it to docs and the onboarding checklist.

Alex (CSM): Great — I'll update the customer that we recommend client changes + we'll do a temporary quota bump as a stopgap. Will follow up after they test. :thumbsup:

Maya (Solutions Eng): One more note — if they use multiple geographic POPs, we should ensure their session id isn't shimmed differently per POP, that can cause duplicate token pools. Priya can add that to the FAQ.

Priya (DevEx): Added note. Also linking internal triage playbook: https://redwood.internal/playbooks/preflight-thundering-herd (internal).

Alex (CSM): Closing loop with customer now. Will post results here once done.

Jordan (Support Eng): Thanks — please @mention me when they finish validation so we can close CR-7894 if not needed.

Alex (CSM): Will do.
